title: Scripting the GUI
tags: GUI
kind: overview
aliases: script scripts
summary: A shell script drives the running GUI through mf gui — it shows files, posts messages, asks a question and waits for your key — and the GUI runs the installed ones for you.


The GUI serves a small HTTP API on the loopback, and `mf gui` is its command line: a
shell script can put a panel in a slot, show a file, post a message, run any GUI
command, and — what makes it more than remote control — ''ask you a question and wait
for your key''. That is how the tagging walks of [[Classifying with tags]] work: the
script decides what to show and what to write, the GUI shows it and collects the
answers.

A script is an ordinary process. It can be started from a terminal while the GUI is
open, or by the GUI itself — from <<cmd "script:run">> or a `!` line ([[Bash mode]]) —
and then the GUI knows it: it shows in the task bar, owns the workspaces it opens, and
can be stopped with one key ([[Script sessions]]).

* To ''run'' the installed scripts: [[Running shipped scripts]].
* To ''answer'' one — the question bar, the script keys, going back, stopping: [[Script sessions]].
* To ''write'' one: [[Writing a GUI script]], then [[Example: comparing images]] for a complete one to start from.

!! What a script can do

|!Command |!Does |
|[[mf gui repo]] |prints the repository of the focused workspace — what a script binds itself to |
|[[mf gui query]], [[mf gui selected]] |print the query the metarecord list shows, and the checked metarecords — what to act on ([[A query is the scope]]) |
|[[mf gui workspace new]], [[mf gui workspace rm]] |open and close a workspace |
|[[mf gui layout]] |prints or sets which workspace each slot shows |
|[[mf gui view]] |prints or sets the panel type of a slot, and the file a `file` panel shows |
|[[mf gui message]] |posts a message on a status bar |
|[[mf gui progress]] |moves the script's bar in the task bar |
|[[mf gui input]] |asks a question and waits for one key |
|[[mf gui prompt]] |asks for a line of text in the command input, with completion |
|[[mf gui command]] |runs a GUI command, as if typed |
|[[mf gui status]] |prints the GUI's state as JSON |
|[[mf gui bench]] |prints or clears the timings the panels recorded |

Everything else a script does goes through the ordinary `mf` commands, to the daemon:
the GUI is only the screen and the keyboard.

`mf gui` finds the GUI by `gui-port` of [[GUI configuration]] (`--gui-url` or
`METAFOLDER_GUI_URL` override it) and sends the GUI's session token, which it reads as
the same user ([[Session tokens]]). The API answers for as long as the GUI runs,
whether or not the daemon does.

